\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Refining independent flux planes}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Under the hypotheses of \ref{fdip-01CB}, choose a product representative of the scalar gauge class and refine all periods by the same positive integer #{q}. In each coordinate plane construct the fine scalar links by \ref{fdip-01BW} and pull them back to the full fine grid. Use the same ordered directions and grading and the same dimensionless mass #{a'M'=aM}. The refined field may also be changed by a scalar site gauge. Then
##{\operatorname{ind}_{\Gamma,M'}(RU)=\operatorname{ind}_{\Gamma,M}(U).}}

\proof{\p{In each plane its scalar flux is unchanged and its maximum absolute plaquette angle decreases by the factor #{q^2}, by \ref{fdip-01BW}. All mixed plaquettes stay trivial. The period-independent window of \ref{fdip-01CB} therefore applies at the same mass on both grids. The even periods remain even and at least #{4}, so their free corner sums agree by \ref{fdip-019Y}. The signed spin multiplicity is the same because the spin-fiber is fixed. Substitution in the product formula proves the equality for every such choice. Scalar site gauges preserve each index by \ref{fdip-0155}.}}
